{"item_id": "ep09e94db6", "set": "main", "subset": "public_unused", "first_episode": false, "arm": "D", "k": 1800, "when": "t2", "agent": "opus-5.5", "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "now_rel": 1288, "t_on_rel": 573, "archive_len": 1289, "L": 894, "channels_n": 2, "raw_used": 1289, "metrics": {"onset": ["point", 595], "exact": {"60": "hit", "120": "hit", "180": "hit"}, "calibration": {"raw_covered": true, "class": "有依据"}, "early_evidence": false, "signed_error_s": 22}, "system_sha256": "38db2bc1dfce43962a3c4b39663366ee200bf2bfb18cf75f7df4826afc37894d", "user_sha256": "af5e88b0a03f81f52ec4d7ed81e073eb0447ba9cc55802eea4f69d9cd7765ebd", "system_text": "You are the historical-analysis assistant of a monitoring system for a laboratory batch distillation plant. The plant records 18 sensor channels once per second. You are called at one moment, \"now\", just after the system flagged that a channel is deviating from its normal range. Your one task: find WHEN the current anomaly first became observable in the flagged channel's recorded readings.\n\nDefinitions\n- The archive is the recorded data from second 0 (the start of this record) up to now. Nothing after now exists for you. Second 0 is either the start of the plant's operation phase or the moment after which the plant had returned to normal; treat it as the beginning of the record.\n- The onset is the first second at which the flagged channel's recorded readings show the anomaly, as a process expert reading the data would judge it. It is not the moment the physical cause began, which can be earlier and invisible.\n- A batch distillation run drifts by itself (temperatures climb as the light component leaves), so a slow trend is not an anomaly; look for a departure from the run's own prior behaviour.\n\nHow your answer is scored (fixed in advance)\n- A point answer (one second) is correct if it lies within ±120 s of the expert's onset second (also reported at ±60 s and ±180 s).\n- An interval [start, end] is correct if it con